    "turn_text": "I would say, you know, after me, of course, but no, it was what a special night. And just I'll answer your question in a second. But like you said, I did get to take my nine-year-old son, Harrison, to the game. And it's only a second NFL game, and he got to watch real history. I mean, Drew Brees passing both Brett Favre and then just about another 30 yards later, past Peyton Manning, to become the all-time passing leader. What a special moment. And he probably doesn't realize as much now how big of a deal it is, but later he will. And Drew is such a wonderful person in the community, does so much back home. I would say, you know what I mean? This has a personal touch for me, but I would say Congressman Brad Wenstrup, from Ohio. Just a hard worker, kind of understated. You don't see him on national press a lot, but I didn't know so many of the things in his background until he literally saved my life after the shooting. And he does it in a humble way. You wouldn't know talking to him just all the things. I mean, he served in Iraq, saved so many lives there as a combat surgeon, as a doctor. by trade and then ultimately decided to run for Congress just because he wanted to help people. And he just works hard. He just got on the Ways and Means Committee and wasn't the first pick or the person expected to get on the Ways and Means Committee when he got that open seat. And so I would say in a lot of ways he's got that real unassuming humble, but just servant leader type of",
