    "turn_text": "some catharsis. Now, I know you don't want to be defined by that event. So you were back in the saddle now, back to work. Energetic, trying to whip the votes. Now, we should stay for the listeners to make clear that whip is not just a noun, it is often a verb. Your job is to whip the vote, to try and get your members in line, to try and figure out what the count is. And my question for you, having covered the House Republican leadership for many years, It strikes me that your job has to be a hell of a lot easier now with Donald Trump in the White House than it was with the Democrat in the White House because a lot of your more conservative members were much trickier to get in line when it was having them decide with John Boehner or just with Paul Ryan. But now with Donald Trump in the White House, he is such an imposing force. It seems like a lot of these guys are easier to bring under your wing. How would you describe the difference between whipping votes under Democratic administration versus whipping votes with Donald Trump as",
