    "turn_idx": 98,
    "turn_text": "Well, I do think that if you look at our country, we've gone through different pendulums swings where, you know, the country's never been completely unified in the sense that, I mean, after September 11th, you saw a real unification where people came together and there have been inflection points like that. But, I mean, you can even look when George H.W. Bush was president right after the Gulf War, he was at a 91% popularity, and just a few years later, he was not president. And he was voted out of office. So the country moves around depending on what's happening in the country at the time. And I don't think that's going to change. I think what's important is, are we confronting the problems that the country's facing today, which are different than they were? You go look, I mean, I have an office in my office suite in the capital, in the website office that Abraham Lincoln used to I mean, there's a fireplace right where Abraham Lincoln used to sit. That's not part of my office. Just think of the problems he had to confront. He chose to finally take on the one big scar of the U.S. Constitution, and that was a never resolved slavery. And he said, I'm going to do something about it. It's time we ended. And he ultimately gave his life for it. We were a divided nation back then, much more divided than we are today. Sure, there's divisions today. But then you can look at different pieces and parts of our history over time where we were even more divided. And maybe you can find times where we were less divided, but even then it might not have lasted long. Ultimately, I think what's most important is how you express those disagreements. I think the civility is one of the things I'm concerned about. You talked about Hillary Clinton. Hillary Clinton just earlier this week was almost encouraging some of the violence you're seeing against people based on their political views.",
